---
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/blog";
import { profile } from "../../data/profile";
const post = posts.find(
  (p) => p.slug === "why-swift-is-the-next-big-thing-for-iot",
)!;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BlogLayout
  title={`${post.title}, Lilly Seay`}
  description={post.description}
  image={post.cover}
>
  <article class="post prose-post">
    <header class="post-head wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Blog · From my FOSDEM 2025 talk</p>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p class="post-meta">
        By {profile.name} · {post.date} · {post.readTime}
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        I gave this talk in the Swift devroom at FOSDEM 2025 in Brussels. This
        is the written version, with the demo video and the code, for anyone who
        would rather read it than watch it. The short version: Embedded Swift
        let me write firmware for a microcontroller in the same language I use
        to build iOS apps, and that changes what a small team can build.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap post-body">
      <figure class="video-figure reveal">
        <video
          controls
          playsinline
          preload="metadata"
          poster={`${base}/blog/swift-iot/motion-light-poster.jpg`}
        >
          <source
            src={`${base}/blog/swift-iot/motion-light-demo.mp4`}
            type="video/mp4"
          />
          Your browser does not support the video tag.
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          The demo: walk past the sensor and the LED turns on. The Home app
          updates at the same moment, and it works the other way too.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Two worlds that felt miles apart</h2>
      <p>
        In 2016 I graduated with a degree in electrical engineering and started
        working full time in hardware design and embedded systems. That is where
        I started working on IoT, the Internet of Things, which connects
        everyday objects to the internet to make them smarter and more
        interactive. Take a simple example: a smart light that turns on when it
        detects motion and integrates seamlessly with the Home app.
      </p>
      <p>
        That kind of engineering was rewarding, but I wanted to expand into
        modern software development, specifically iOS. My days were spent
        writing low-level C for embedded systems. At night I was writing Swift,
        building my first iOS app.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the time, those two worlds felt miles apart. I remember coding in C
        and worrying that a single typo could crash my program or even damage my
        hardware. That constant caution made Swift's modern features, type
        safety, automatic memory management, and interoperability with C, even
        more exciting. They hinted at a language that could bring the two worlds
        together.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fast forward to 2024. Embedded Swift was announced, and suddenly my two
        worlds didn't just overlap. They intersected.
      </p>

      <h2>What Embedded Swift is</h2>
      <p>
        Traditionally, embedded developers rely on C and C++. They are precise,
        powerful languages that offer full control over hardware. That precision
        comes with challenges: manual memory management, complex error handling,
        and a steep learning curve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Embedded Swift is a compilation mode of Swift built for
        microcontrollers. It keeps the parts of the language that make code safe
        and readable, value and reference types, optionals, error handling,
        generics, and drops the parts that need a large runtime. The result has
        a small enough footprint to run on a chip, and it is already used inside
        Apple's own devices for kernel-level and security-critical code. For
        hardware work it brings modern, safe, and scalable code to a place that
        has rarely had it.
      </p>

      <h2>The build</h2>
      <p>
        I built a motion-activated smart light. A motion sensor detects movement
        and sends a high signal to an ESP32-C6. That signal flips the LED on or
        off, and the change shows up in the Home app.
      </p>
      <ul class="kit">
        <li><strong>Board:</strong> ESP32-C6</li>
        <li><strong>Sensor:</strong> a PIR motion sensor on GPIO pin 5</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Protocol:</strong> Matter, so the Home app can see the device no
          matter who made it
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Language:</strong> Embedded Swift, on top of the ESP-IDF C libraries
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I started from Apple's Matter example in Swift, the one featured in the
        WWDC24 session
        <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2024/10197/"
          >Go small with Embedded Swift</a
        >. Everything below is the part I added for the motion sensor. The LED
        and Matter setup come from that example, and the full project is in the
        <a href="https://github.com/apple/swift-matter-examples"
          >swift-matter-examples</a
        > repo.
      </p>

      <h2>Reading the sensor</h2>
      <p>
        The first step happens in the bridging header. I added the GPIO driver
        for the sensor, the interrupt headers, and a Matter C++ call that
        updates the Home app. That is the C and C++ interoperability doing its
        job: the ESP-IDF drivers stay in C, and I call them from Swift.
      </p>
      <p>
        The core of the sensor lives in a MotionSensor class. The initializer
        does two things: it configures the GPIO pin so the board can receive
        input whenever motion is detected, and it sets up a thread-safe
        interrupt handler for that pin. When the interrupt fires, the LED state
        updates. That is the whole connection between sensor input and hardware
        output.
      </p>
      <pre><code>final class MotionSensor &#123;
    fileprivate let pin: Int32
    private var motionDetectedCallback: (() -&gt; Void)?
    fileprivate var interruptQueue: OpaquePointer?

    init(pin: Int32, onMotionDetected: @escaping () -&gt; Void) &#123;
        self.pin = pin
        self.motionDetectedCallback = onMotionDetected

        if let error = setupGPIO() &#123;
            print("GPIO setup failed: \(error.description)")
            esp_restart()
        &#125;

        if let error = setupTask() &#123;
            print("Task setup failed: \(error.description)")
            esp_restart()
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        Configuring the pin is a call into the C driver with a Swift struct. The
        pin is set as an input with a pull-down resistor, and the interrupt
        fires on the rising edge, which is the moment the sensor sees motion.
      </p>
      <pre><code>private func setupGPIO() -&gt; GPIOError? &#123;
    var config = gpio_config_t(
        pin_bit_mask: 1 &lt;&lt; UInt64(pin),
        mode: gpio_mode_t(GPIO_MODE_INPUT.rawValue),
        pull_up_en: gpio_pullup_t(GPIO_PULLUP_DISABLE.rawValue),
        pull_down_en: gpio_pulldown_t(GPIO_PULLDOWN_ENABLE.rawValue),
        intr_type: gpio_int_type_t(GPIO_INTR_POSEDGE.rawValue)
    )

    if gpio_config(&amp;config) != ESP_OK &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .configurationFailed(pin: pin))
    &#125;
    if gpio_install_isr_service(0) != ESP_OK &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .isrServiceInstallationFailed)
    &#125;
    if gpio_isr_handler_add(
        gpio_num_t(pin),
        globalGPIOISRHandler,
        UnsafeMutableRawPointer(Unmanaged.passUnretained(self).toOpaque())
    ) != ESP_OK &#123;
        return GPIOError(code: .isrHandlerAdditionFailed(pin: pin))
    &#125;
    return nil
&#125;</code></pre>

      <h2>Error handling that reads like Swift</h2>
      <p>
        One thing I appreciate about Swift is its structured approach to error
        handling. If any part of the configuration fails, setting up the pin,
        installing the interrupt service, creating the queue, the function
        returns a specific error instead of a status code you have to remember
        to check. I defined those cases in a small extension.
      </p>
      <pre><code>extension MotionSensor &#123;
    struct GPIOError &#123;
        enum Code &#123;
            case configurationFailed(pin: Int32)
            case isrServiceInstallationFailed
            case isrHandlerAdditionFailed(pin: Int32)
            case interruptQueueCreationFailed
            case interruptTaskCreationFailed
        &#125;

        let code: Code

        var description: String &#123;
            switch code &#123;
            case .configurationFailed(let pin):
                return "Failed to configure GPIO pin \(pin)."
            case .isrServiceInstallationFailed:
                return "Failed to install GPIO ISR service."
            case .isrHandlerAdditionFailed(let pin):
                return "Failed to add ISR handler for GPIO pin \(pin)."
            case .interruptQueueCreationFailed:
                return "Failed to create interrupt queue."
            case .interruptTaskCreationFailed:
                return "Failed to create interrupt task."
            &#125;
        &#125;
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        Back in the initializer, an <code>if let</code> catches any of those errors
        and restarts the ESP32 to recover. On a device that has to run unattended,
        handling failures at runtime instead of hoping they never happen is the difference
        between a demo and a product.
      </p>

      <h2>Keeping the Home app in sync</h2>
      <p>
        After the sensor detects motion, the LED state has to reach the Home
        app. In Matter.swift I extended the existing Matter code with a
        function, <code>reportLightState</code>, that takes the current LED
        state and pushes it into the Matter stack. Then in Main.swift I created
        the MotionSensor on GPIO pin 5 and gave it a closure. A closure keeps
        the detection logic easy to read: when the interrupt fires, the LED
        toggles and the new state is reported.
      </p>
      <pre><code>let motionSensor = MotionSensor(pin: 5) &#123;
    led.enabled.toggle()
    lightEndpoint.reportLightState(to: led.enabled)
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
        Set the board target, build, flash it to the ESP32-C6, and monitor.
        Every LED state change shows up in the terminal logs, and the light in
        the Home app flips with it. The result is a fully functional smart light
        that responds to motion and stays in sync with the Home app.
      </p>

      <h2>Why this matters</h2>
      <p>
        Embedded Swift has changed the way I do IoT development. It prevents
        memory leaks with automatic memory management, avoids error-prone code
        with built-in safety features, and it is easier for me to read. Its
        small memory footprint makes it a fit for IoT devices.
      </p>
      <p>
        Swift's potential doesn't stop at the microcontroller. It now connects
        every part of an IoT product: the embedded system, the back end, and the
        apps. One language across the stack simplifies development, removes
        barriers for startups and indie developers, and makes efficient,
        scalable, user-friendly products possible for small teams.
      </p>
      <p>And that is why Swift is the next big thing for IoT.</p>

      <section class="post-outro reveal">
        <h2>Watch the talk</h2>
        <p>
          The recording and slides are on the FOSDEM archive:
          <a
            href="https://archive.fosdem.org/2025/schedule/event/fosdem-2025-6148-why-swift-is-the-next-big-thing-for-iot/"
            >talk page</a
          > and
          <a
            href="https://video.fosdem.org/2025/k4401/fosdem-2025-6148-why-swift-is-the-next-big-thing-for-iot.mp4"
            >video</a
          >. If you are building something that needs an app and a device to
          talk to each other, <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}
            >send me a note</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
